ref 17592186049716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050622 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050622 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050622 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050622 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050622 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Two-Variable Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050622 #edb/ref 1033 356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050622 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "59bac138-6475-51d1-971d-3bd216b92f3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098983 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098983 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098983 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186509809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098983 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1be58fdc-55c3-5738-97fc-4b0fc03a148f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098989 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098989 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098989 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098989 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098989 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098989 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098989 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098989 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186509888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098989 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6c302848-acd9-5af2-94b7-b0a147f47676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098995 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098995 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098995 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098995 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098995 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098995 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098995 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098995 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186509732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098995 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ae5cdce9-4bc0-5a63-9f3a-1ec87a9fb050" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100650 #edb/ref 1069 "e-351" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100650 #edb/ref 1071 "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base but no lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $27\\pi\\, \\textrm{in}^3.$ What is the radius of the cylinder that gives the smallest surface area?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100650 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7f66eb5-8151-5886-9b35-58632319651f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100650 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/ad/adb9c56132e8f346a07a9a644cd364c2347ad8c551a3c9bd4842b7067eb1d050.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS= 2\\pi r h + \\pi r^2.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $27\\pi \\, \\textrm{in}^3,$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n27\\pi = \\pi r^2 h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh = \\dfrac{27}{ r^2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nS(r)& = 2\\pi r \\left(\\dfrac{27}{ r^2}\\right) + \\pi r^2\\\n & = \\dfrac{54\\pi}{r} +  \\pi r^2.}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\nS'(r) = -\\dfrac{54\\pi}{r^2} + 2\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{54\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 2\\pi r & =\\frac{54\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{3} & =54\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\ r & =3\\,\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r <   3$ then $S'(r) <         0,$ and if $r > 3$ then $S'(r) > 0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=3$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r =3\\,\\textrm{in}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100651 #edb/ref 1069 "e-352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100651 #edb/ref 1071 "We need to create a cylindrical tin with no lid using $25\\pi\\,\\mathrm{cm^2}$ of sheet metal. What is the radius $r$ of the cylinder that has the largest volume? \n\n![](images/a2/a2916167b3c8d4169339280fe67a0018b5d09a9784260cf6488cd6825753b2b9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100651 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c82b0d87-792b-57d2-9340-9095af643c44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100651 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $25\\pi\\,\\mathrm{cm^2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi r h + \\pi r^2 = 25\\pi.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =25\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{25\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{25-{r}^{2}}{2r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\times \\frac{25-{r}^{2}}{2r} \\\\ V(r) & =\\frac{(25r-{r}^{3})\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n \n\n$$\nV'(r) = \\frac{(25 - 3 r^2)\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{(25-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2} & =0 \\\\ 25 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root in the last step because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r   = \\dfrac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3}$  is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nV''(r) = -3\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that $V''\\left( \\dfrac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right) < 0.$ So, the stationary point $r=\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\, \\textrm{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100783 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7523" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100783 #edb/ref 1071 "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical tank with a base and a lid. The tank must have an exact volume of $18\\pi\\,\\mathrm{m}^3.$\nTo make the tank, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a metal sheet that costs $\\[math]1.50$ per $\\mathrm{m}^2$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a metal sheet that costs $\\[/math]0.50$ per $\\mathrm{m}^2$ for the base and lid.\n\nWhat is the radius of the tank that gives the smallest possible manufacturing cost?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100783 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d2657d6-3dc6-5855-8464-4f23dc675211" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100783 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/d7/d72b9a87e460f841bbd135fd16806607c16aa0b37b2279727bd7d9eeeb9c6982.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 1.5+2\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $18\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^3,$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n18\\pi = \\pi r^2 h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constant equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh = \\dfrac{18}{r^2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =3\\pi r(\\frac{18}{{r}^{2}})+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{54\\pi }{r}+\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\nC'(r) = -\\dfrac{54\\pi}{r^2} + 2\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{54\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 2\\pi r & =\\frac{54\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{3} & =54\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\ r & =3\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r < 3$ then $C'(r) <    0,$ and if $r > 3$ then $C'(r) > 0,$ which means that the stationary point $r = 3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the tank with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r = 3\\,\\textrm{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100840 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100840 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100840 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100840 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100840 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100840 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100840 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100840 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100840 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100840 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100840 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100840 #edb/ref 1039 "Minimizing the Surface Area of a Cylinder With a Fixed Volume" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100840 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100840 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "39738d1a-2b58-58e7-8b93-fae76501045c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100841 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100841 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100841 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100841 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100841 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100841 #edb/ref 1039 "Maximizing the Volume of a Cylinder With a Fixed Surface Area" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100841 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100841 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a8fae559-f88f-50ec-a1f0-a7cfeb319d19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100945 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100945 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100945 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100945 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100945 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100945 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100945 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100945 #edb/ref 1039 "Optimizing the Cost of Building a Cylinder" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100945 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100945 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a8ac7b46-da1d-57f9-ab61-efa672ca8128" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101069 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101069 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101069 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101069 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101069 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101069 #edb/ref 1045 "Optimization Problems Involving Cylinders" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101069 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101069 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101069 #edb/ref 1050 1380.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101069 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "55f9d198-0e40-5068-b3d7-cb97445bd35c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101277 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101277 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18444" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101277 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101277 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/50/50853fcaa44ff9b7d80e708e546275286541afc0b6ed05c4ca87ea3175364c09.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with no lid using $48\\pi \\,{cm}^{2}$ of sheet metal. What is the largest possible volume of the cylinder?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101277 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28541c18-bc9f-5800-8a64-d3d3ece27f6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101277 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101277 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $48\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}=48\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =48\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{48\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{48-{r}^{2}}{2r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\times \\frac{48-{r}^{2}}{2r} \\\\ V(r) & =\\frac{(48r-{r}^{3})\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=\\frac{(48-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{(48-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2} & =0 \\\\ 48 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =16 \\\\ r & =4\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=4$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=\\frac{-6\\pi r}{2}=-3\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(4)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=4$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nSo the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=4\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nFinally, to find the largest volume, we substitute $r=4\\,\\text{cm}$ into the equation for the volume $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(4) & =\\frac{(48(4)-(4)^{3})\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(192-64)\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =64\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the largest possible volume of the cylinder is $V=64\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101278 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101278 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18446" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101278 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101278 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/38/38883c7cc4bfe8c9ecccadfcc2d0c75ff66f79b19ff697c15ddbe53f4c63dbab.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with a lid using $216\\pi \\,{cm}^{2}$ of sheet metal. What is the radius $r$ of the cylinder that has the largest volume?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101278 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b58094e-e8c0-5c82-ac8b-027a5c669c62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101278 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101278 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $216\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}=216\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =216\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{216\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{108-{r}^{2}}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\times \\frac{108-{r}^{2}}{r} \\\\ V(r) & =(108r-{r}^{3})\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=(108-3{r}^{2})\\pi .\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ (108-3{r}^{2})\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 108 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=6$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=-6\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(6)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=6$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=6\\,\\text{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101279 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101279 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18447" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101279 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101279 #edb/ref 1071 "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base but no lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $64\\pi \\,{mm}^{3}.$ What is the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101279 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1317ed44-314d-54c1-9f68-756c95a13928" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101279 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101279 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/ce/cec96bdd2335ff5635d11c4ea6016257b35bd5450778a70ecc1f5e0156ccfe9e.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $64\\pi \\,{mm}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n64\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{64}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{64}{{r}^{2}})+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{r}+\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{128\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{128\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 2\\pi r & =\\frac{128\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{3} & =128\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =64 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ r & =4\\,\\text{mm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<4$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>4$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=4$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nSo the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r=4\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area, we substitute $r=4\\,\\text{mm}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(4) & =\\frac{128\\pi }{4}+\\pi (4)^{2} \\\\  & =32\\pi +16\\pi  \\\\  & =48\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder is $S=48\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101627 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101627 #edb/ref 1061 641455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101627 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c14aa67-65c7-52ad-b74d-223f276e9735" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101627 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186102011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101628 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101628 #edb/ref 1061 641456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101628 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "878a1892-1997-5956-bc6f-f3f8903e7276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101628 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101629 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101629 #edb/ref 1061 641460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101629 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c19129d0-cc7e-5b81-b68a-0b24932487c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101629 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101788 #edb/ref 1061 641465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101788 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7dabe378-eb14-5a04-a969-d7336f1d4578" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101788 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102011 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to construct a cylindrical container with a fixed volume, yet we wish to use as little material as possible to minimize our production costs. \n\n![](images/33/339a377a68f93c78cf99b0f58333ab26c24bff91509dd707d22981d773ef077c.png)\n\nThis is yet another type of optimization problem. Let's remind ourselves of the general strategy for optimization problems.\n\n1\\. Draw a diagram of the situation, introducing variables where necessary.\n\n2\\. Write down the equation of the quantity that you're trying to maximize or minimize. This is the objective function, and it might include more than one variable.\n\n3\\. Write down the constraint equation.\n\n4\\. Use the constraint equation to write the objective function in terms of a single variable only.\n\n5\\. Differentiate the objective function, set it equal to zero, and solve for the stationary points.\n\n6\\. Test each stationary point using the second derivative test. Or you can use the first derivative test if it's easier.\n\nIn the following examples, we'll need to use the formulas for a cylindrical can's volume $V$ and surface area $S.$ For a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h,$ its volume is given by the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h.\n$$\n\nIf the cylinder is closed at both ends, its surface area $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S=2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the cylinder is closed at only one end, its surface area $S$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S=2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102011 #edb/ref 1065 672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102011 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "67df391f-27a3-55c3-bc1d-46e69a2931e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102011 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509713 #edb/ref 1080 "7\\,\\text{ft}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509713 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509713 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0abcfa87-c2d8-55b9-8b6c-f6e6dc2c2e01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509732 #edb/ref 1080 "48\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509732 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509732 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1812698a-baa5-5c04-bbe5-0eaab253aef4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509734 #edb/ref 1080 "9\\,\\text{ft}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186509734 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 1759