2 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510355 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510355 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510355 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e9f0d32e-11c5-5d16-b8fb-323736868b33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510374 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510374 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510374 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510374 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ae/aefac5e84b9ea13ce039e4e2e04fda830be9ab9c17a62665ba5e5d3a7b7f6f68.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with no lid using $108\\pi \\,{cm}^{2}$ of a metal sheet. What is the radius $r$ of the cylinder that has the largest volume?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510374 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c38e997-645f-5cbf-9057-08f6bff2619b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510374 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $108\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}=108\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =108\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{108\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{108-{r}^{2}}{2r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\times \\frac{108-{r}^{2}}{2r} \\\\ V(r) & =\\frac{(108r-{r}^{3})\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=\\frac{(108-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{(108-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2} & =0 \\\\ 108 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=6$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=\\frac{-6\\pi r}{2}=-3\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(6)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=6$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=6\\,\\text{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
